The genre crystallized in the subterranean transit arteries of late-1950s post-industrial hubs, specifically aboard the overnight Treno del Sonámbulo. Insomniac night-shift workers, exiled jazz brass players, and rogue radio technicians began assembling in the baggage cars. Carrying modified battery-powered pocket oscillators alongside traditional 1950s valve-amplified trombones, these commuters sought to neutralize the maddening rattle of the rails. They draped the steel cars in velvet parlor curtains, burned dried copal resin, and engaged in all-night "clack-jamming." The aesthetic is strictly "Retro-Commuter Gothic": tweed suits stained with machine oil, brass-plated sleep-goggles, pocket synths housed in vintage cigarette cases, and hand-carved wooden guacharacas filled with lead bearings. 

